1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
When Python starts a thread inside Pythago, how does its native call reach Go? The answer starts before the program runs.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Bao edits the binary references. A pthread wrapper chooses who starts the thread. Small thunks handle calling conventions and thread-local addresses.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:31,000
We will follow a pthread_create call through those layers, then see which parts are shared by Linux, macOS, and Windows.

4
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:43,000
A relocatable object groups machine instructions and data into sections. A symbol defines a location in one section, or names something another input must supply.

5
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:55,000
A relocation tells the linker where to apply a fixup, what kind of calculation to use, and which symbol it refers to when one is needed.

6
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:08,000
In this simplified relative call, the symbol is at address two thousand and the next instruction is at one thousand. The displacement is one thousand bytes.

7
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:20,000
A shared library already has a layout and may carry dynamic fixups. Bao can translate the load image and carry forward the fixups it still needs.

8
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Think of a call as a reference to a symbol. Wrapping changes that reference from pthread_create to the glue function named __wrap_pthread_create.

9
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Inside the wrapper, the reference named __real_pthread_create becomes a reference to pthread_create. It can then resolve to the system implementation.

10
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:57,000
A rename changes a spelling. A prefix groups a component’s own definitions and references under distinct names. Unrelated imports keep their names.

11
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Localization makes a definition local to its object. Stripping can remove unused names, but references must stay valid. An edited archive needs an updated symbol index.

12
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:22,000
For an undefined global symbol on the wrap list, Bao changes the name to the wrapper name. The relocation still has a symbol entry to refer to.

13
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:35,000
If the same object defines the wrapped symbol, Bao keeps that definition. It adds or reuses a wrapper symbol and retargets runtime relocations.

14
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:48,000
The real-name case goes the other way. An undefined __real_ reference is renamed to the original name. It resolves to the original definition at link time.

15
00:02:48,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The normal shared build starts with the target’s libpython and a C glue object. The generator gives Bao the four pthread symbols that it wants to wrap.

16
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:14,000
On Linux, a call can go through a procedure linkage table stub and its target pointer, in the global offset table. Bao binds that import slot to the wrapper.

17
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:27,000
The generated dist package contains Go-linkable native objects and registration code. The Go linker places the image. Initialization resolves remaining imports and runs native constructors.

18
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:40,000
The Linux LTO route starts from bitcode. LLVM lowers it to native objects and lld wraps the relocatable references before Bao translates the resulting archive.

19
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:52,000
The generator sets the glue object and wrap list together. It selects a dylib on macOS and a shared object on Linux, then calls the Bao build.

20
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Bao finds the undefined global imports with the requested name. The example shows the ELF shared-object path after Bao has read the image’s relocations.

21
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:17,000
The edit requires matching glue. Bao rejects a missing wrapper instead of leaving an unresolved hook. Otherwise it gives the imported symbol the wrapper name.

22
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:29,000
In the LTO route, the partial link uses the same wrap list. The link step makes a relocatable object, which Pythago packages with the symbol table for Bao.

23
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:41,000
At runtime, CPython calls its usual pthread function. The edited reference sends it to C glue. The wrapper still has the original pthread calling convention.

24
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:54,000
The wrapper checks for a default stack and asks the spawn hook to handle the call. Success and positive pthread errors are returned to the caller.

25
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:07,000
If the hook is unavailable or the stack size needs libc, the wrapper calls the real pthread function. The forked child disables the Go spawn hook.

26
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:20,000
The C shim tracks the pthread identities it owns. Join and detach use that registry, and ordinary threads fall back to libc. Exit needs its own wrapper.

27
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:32,000
On the dedicated initialization thread, Go registers a C-callable callback for threadStart. C glue keeps the pointer and the target number of spare threads.

28
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:45,000
In the wrapper, the greater-than-or-equal-to-zero test is deliberate. Zero is success and a positive error, such as EAGAIN at the cap, stays an error.

29
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:57,000
If no hook is installed, the C spawn function returns negative one. That is a private signal to use the real pthread implementation.

30
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:09,000
threadStart starts a goroutine and returns. Inside the goroutine, Go locks to the operating-system thread and enters the C thread main function.

31
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Creation first assigns a thread record to a waiting spare if one is available. Otherwise C calls the Go spawn hook to start a locked goroutine.

32
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:34,000
The child publishes pthread_self and enters the registry. It signals started, so the parent can return the handle before the Python function has finished.

33
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:47,000
The C entry point runs CPython’s thread function on that operating-system thread. When the function returns, C publishes done and waits for release.

34
00:06:47,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Join waits for done and releases the record. Detach grants release without waiting for completion. This preserves the thread’s identity and lifetime.

35
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:13,000
On the special pthread_exit path, the Go-backed thread parks. Calling the system exit there would force an unwind across Go frames. Ordinary threads still use libc.

36
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:25,000
A thunk is a small piece of executable code that adapts one interface to another. In a Go-to-C call, Pythago packs the target and arguments into a frame.

37
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:38,000
The Go runtime enters the C call thunk on the system stack. On Linux and macOS using amd64, six integer slots go in registers and two go on the stack.

38
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:51,000
The native callee can call back into Go and cause the goroutine stack to move. The thunk adjusts the saved frame pointer before writing the return value.

39
00:07:51,000 --> 00:08:04,000
The reverse path starts at a C trampoline. It builds a call frame, identifies the registered Go function, and enters the dispatcher through the platform callback bridge.

40
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:16,000
On amd64 the thunk reads the function address and the first six integer arguments from the frame. It puts the last two integer slots on the system stack.

41
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:29,000
After the native call, the thunk saves the result. It compares the goroutine stack top with its earlier value, then stores the result in the corrected frame.

42
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:42,000
In the other direction, the C fastcall entry builds a frame from the Python arguments and the function index. That index selects the Go callable.

43
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:55,000
The trampoline enters Go through crosscall2 on Linux and macOS, and through a Go callback on Windows. On return it hands Python the result or the recorded exception.

44
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:08,000
A native thread-local variable has the same name in the code on every thread. Its address can be different on each operating-system thread.

45
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:21,000
The translated Linux image has a TLS template. Bao’s runtime finds or creates an instance for the calling thread. The variable’s offset within the instance is shared.

46
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:34,000
For the Linux descriptor example, Bao builds a pair: the resolver entry point and the variable’s offset in the template. Native code calls the resolver.

47
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:47,000
On amd64, the thunk returns an offset from the current thread pointer. The caller combines the two to reach that thread’s variable.

48
00:09:47,000 --> 00:10:00,000
A cached instance can be found quickly. A miss calls the TLS helper that can allocate a fresh instance. Register preservation follows the descriptor convention.

49
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Bao classifies supported native TLS relocations as it reads the image. In the descriptor case, the dynamic fixup becomes an internal descriptor relocation.

50
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:26,000
When Bao lowers the descriptor, it stores the template offset in the second word. A relocation in the first word points to the resolver thunk.

51
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:39,000
The amd64 fast path checks that the cached record belongs to the calling thread. It adds the template offset to the block address, then subtracts the thread pointer.

52
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:52,000
This is a separate use of a thunk. It resolves a storage location under the TLS descriptor convention. A C call thunk moves arguments, and a callback trampoline dispatches code.

53
00:10:52,000 --> 00:11:04,000
On Linux, Bao translates the ELF image from libpython. System imports still bind to shared libraries, and Bao supplies native TLS support for the translated image.

54
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:17,000
On macOS, Bao translates a Mach-O dylib. Its bind fixups can target the glue wrappers. Thread-local variables use Mach-O descriptors and Bao’s corresponding runtime.

55
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:30,000
On Windows, the dist package embeds the CPython and glue DLLs. Pythago extracts and loads them, and finds exported addresses with GetProcAddress.

56
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:43,000
Windows keeps CPython’s own thread creation. Go uses SyscallN and a Windows callback for ABI crossing. The pthread and ELF descriptor paths are specific to other targets.

57
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:55,000
At build time, a pthread reference moves to the glue wrapper. A real-name reference in the wrapper keeps a path to the system callable.

58
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:07,000
At runtime, the wrapper chooses a locked Go-backed thread or the system implementation, or returns a pthread error. The child publishes its real thread identity.

59
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:20,000
The call thunk, callback trampoline, and TLS resolver each adapt a different interface. Follow the input and the result at each boundary to see which job is being done.
